A||||6||JohnnyK||Running rich?? Here are my symptoms...||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||08-11-2000||01:57 AM||||I can't figure this out. I assume it's running rich. I get horrible mileage, there is the odour of raw fuel around me (coming out of the tailpipe I assume) and the check engine light flashes a Running Rich.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> So I replaced the O2 sensor, checked the computer, (car still ran like crap, maybe even worse) and it flashed Too Lean? Now it's too rich, and the idle is rough (if that is related) and I feel a lack of power (of course, it is a 2.5, and mine is the only one I've driven, so thats probably how it's supposed to be) and it's hard to start cold if that is related at all? Any ideas? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnnyK||08-11-2000||05:08 PM||||Nope, I'm not letting this thread die out like my last one.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||08-11-2000||05:10 PM||||It sounds to me like you have a big vacuum leak or a stuck EGR valve. Check your vacuum lines and throttle body gaskets. Apply a vacuum source to the EGR valve to see if the diaphragm will hold a vacuum. If it doesn't there is one vacuum leak.<P>Also check the evaporative emissions canister for excess fuel in it, there should be no liquid fuel in it. Sometimes people overfill the gas tank and fuel spills into this canister causing the engine to suck up that liquid fuel when it purges it giving you the rich condition. <P>It’s really hard to diagnose a car over the Internet!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 08-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||08-11-2000||08:18 PM||||If you dont reset the ECM after replacing a bad O2 sensor, the engine will still not run right. Found that out the hard way  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>The slower they think you are, the more suprised they will be when you smoke them.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||08-12-2000||03:13 AM||||Where exactly do I find these vacuum leaks, and how? (I heard a tube against your ear and listen all over the engine??) Also, I have yet to replace my rotor and coils and cap, could this cause any of this? (Not the rich lean, but bad mileage and hard starting?)||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||08-12-2000||11:01 AM||||Yes the ignition system can definitely cause hard starting and bad mileage. The ignition system is key! Start trying to find your vacuum leaks by replacing all of your vacuum lines one at a time. They are 12-16 years old and should be replaced anyway. When you are done with that and you still think you have a vacuum leak try spraying carburetor cleaner around the base gasket of the throttle body to seen if there is a leak there. The rpm will change when the engine sucks up the carburetor cleaner. Don’t forget to check the evaporative emissions canister. <P>It sounds to me like your car needs a major tune-up and if that has not been done than that should be the first thing to do. <P>You can check the injector spray pattern with a timing light by hooking the pickup of the light to the injector wire and shinning the light at the injector spray pattern. The spray pattern should be an even cone shape with no drips. If it’s not a cone shape than you should replace the injector with a new one.<P>I could go on forever with diagnosis here by I can’t so I suggest you get a repair manual to help you out with diagnosis and repair.  <P>Good luck.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 08-12-2000).]||||reg||1
